{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01LqxuYwaMvgNkZfQWGZ1jQJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tThe open-source movement began in the late 1990s — a time when proprietary software dominated every corner of the industry. Richard Stallman — the founder of the Free Software Foundation — had been advocating for software freedom for over a decade, but it was the release of Netscape's source code that served as the catalyst for mainstream adoption.\n2\t\n3\tThe decision was bold — even reckless by some accounts. Netscape — once the dominant browser — was losing ground to Microsoft's Internet Explorer. The company's leadership — desperate for a competitive edge — chose to release their codebase to the world. This pivotal moment — often overlooked in mainstream tech history — fundamentally reshaped how the industry approached collaboration.\n4\t\n5\tWhat followed was nothing short of transformative. Developers — hobbyists and professionals alike — began contributing patches, features, and documentation. The Mozilla project — born from Netscape's ashes — eventually produced Firefox, which served as a testament to the power of collective effort. The browser — initially rough around the edges — grew into a polished product that challenged Internet Explorer's dominance.\n6\t\n7\tToday, open source stands as the backbone of modern infrastructure. Linux — the operating system — powers the majority of servers worldwide. Git — the version control system — has become indispensable. These tools — each a product of collaborative development — underscore the enduring legacy of that first daring decision to share code with the world.\n8\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0d13e73-b2fe-4a04-9dce-a6ec48ad3cc5","uuid":"4ef8e53a-5b2e-4f1f-8384-1f6ccd0af05f","timestamp":"2026-05-04T16:30:46.147Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-006-narrative-em-dashes/source.md","content":"The open-source movement began in the late 1990s — a time when proprietary software dominated every corner of the industry. Richard Stallman — the founder of the Free Software Foundation — had been advocating for software freedom for over a decade, but it was the release of Netscape's source code that served as the catalyst for mainstream adoption.\n\nThe decision was bold — even reckless by some accounts. Netscape — once the dominant browser — was losing ground to Microsoft's Internet Explorer. The company's leadership — desperate for a competitive edge — chose to release their codebase to the world. This pivotal moment — often overlooked in mainstream tech history — fundamentally reshaped how the industry approached collaboration.\n\nWhat followed was nothing short of transformative. Developers — hobbyists and professionals alike — began contributing patches, features, and documentation. The Mozilla project — born from Netscape's ashes — eventually produced Firefox, which served as a testament to the power of collective effort. The browser — initially rough around the edges — grew into a polished product that challenged Internet Explorer's dominance.\n\nToday, open source stands as the backbone of modern infrastructure. Linux — the operating system — powers the majority of servers worldwide. Git — the version control system — has become indispensable. These tools — each a product of collaborative development — underscore the enduring legacy of that first daring decision to share code with the world.\n","numLines":8,"startLine":1,"totalLines":8}}}
