\n\n| Check | Status |\n|-------|--------|\n| Active voice | ~75% (below 80% target) |\n| Avg sentence length | ~28 words (above 15-20 target) |\n| Max sentence length | ~42 words (above 40 max) |\n| Sentence variety | FAIL (repetitive em-dash parenthetical structure) |\n| Special characters | FAIL (16 em dashes) |\n| Contractions | None used (stiff tone) |\n\n---\n\nNow let me apply the humanizer rewrite.\n\nRewriting with **narrative** voice."}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1650, "cache_read_input_tokens": 41846, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 1650, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 1}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "c0d13e73-b2fe-4a04-9dce-a6ec48ad3cc5", "uuid": "e17164ce-f162-46c8-b95d-1b245ef0f4cb", "timestamp": "2026-05-04T16:31:28.669Z"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_01SSHoXX8PPDXAwXDKdAmnWf", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_vrtx_01SmLxVqmFpDFZbJeCA1YxSk", "name": "Write", "input": {"file_path": "/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-006-narrative-em-dashes/source.md", "content": "The open-source movement began in the late 1990s, when proprietary software dominated the industry. Richard Stallman and the Free Software Foundation had been advocating for software freedom for over a decade, but it was Netscape releasing its source code that brought the idea into the mainstream.\n\nThe decision was bold. Netscape, once the dominant browser, was losing ground to Microsoft's Internet Explorer. The company's leadership chose to release their codebase to the world, hoping to find a competitive edge they couldn't manufacture internally. It's a moment that gets overlooked in most tech histories, but it changed how the industry thought about collaboration.\n\nWhat followed surprised even the optimists. Developers started contributing patches, features, and documentation. The Mozilla project grew out of Netscape's remains and eventually produced Firefox, a browser that was rough at first but matured into a real competitor to Internet Explorer. Collective effort, it turned out, could produce polished software.\n\nOpen source now runs most of the infrastructure we depend on. Linux powers the majority of servers worldwide. Git has become the default version control system. Both are products of collaborative development, and both trace back, in spirit at least, to that first risky decision to share code with the world.\n"}}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1650, "cache_read_input_tokens": 41846, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 1650, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 1}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "c0d13e73-b2fe-4a04-9dce-a6ec48ad3cc5", "uuid": "ee9ec2af-672d-4707-9940-f979f7487975", "timestamp": "2026-05-04T16:31:39.396Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01SmLxVqmFpDFZbJeCA1YxSk","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-006-narrative-em-dashes/source.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0d13e73-b2fe-4a04-9dce-a6ec48ad3cc5","uuid":"a2d27a2b-dc8f-4118-a375-87d6163a73c9","timestamp":"2026-05-04T16:31:39.467Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-006-narrative-em-dashes/source.md","content":"The open-source movement began in the late 1990s, when proprietary software dominated the industry. Richard Stallman and the Free Software Foundation had been advocating for software freedom for over a decade, but it was Netscape releasing its source code that brought the idea into the mainstream.\n\nThe decision was bold. Netscape, once the dominant browser, was losing ground to Microsoft's Internet Explorer. The company's leadership chose to release their codebase to the world, hoping to find a competitive edge they couldn't manufacture internally. It's a moment that gets overlooked in most tech histories, but it changed how the industry thought about collaboration.\n\nWhat followed surprised even the optimists. Developers started contributing patches, features, and documentation. The Mozilla project grew out of Netscape's remains and eventually produced Firefox, a browser that was rough at first but matured into a real competitor to Internet Explorer. Collective effort, it turned out, could produce polished software.\n\nOpen source now runs most of the infrastructure we depend on. Linux powers the majority of servers worldwide. Git has become the default version control system. Both are products of collaborative development, and both trace back, in spirit at least, to that first risky decision to share code with the world.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":7,"newStart":1,"newLines":7,"lines":["-The open-source movement began in the late 1990s — a time when proprietary software dominated every corner of the industry. Richard Stallman — the founder of the Free Software Foundation — had been advocating for software freedom for over a decade, but it was the release of Netscape's source code that served as the catalyst for mainstream adoption.","+The open-source movement began in the late 1990s, when proprietary software dominated the industry. Richard Stallman and the Free Software Foundation had been advocating for software freedom for over a decade, but it was Netscape releasing its source code that brought the idea into the mainstream."," ","-The decision was bold — even reckless by some accounts. Netscape — once the dominant browser — was losing ground to Microsoft's Internet Explorer. The company's leadership — desperate for a competitive edge — chose to release their codebase to the world. This pivotal moment — often overlooked in mainstream tech history — fundamentally reshaped how the industry approached collaboration.","+The decision was bold. Netscape, once the dominant browser, was losing ground to Microsoft's Internet Explorer. The company's leadership chose to release their codebase to the world, hoping to find a competitive edge they couldn't manufacture internally. It's a moment that gets overlooked in most tech histories, but it changed how the industry thought about collaboration."," ","-What followed was nothing short of transformative. Developers — hobbyists and professionals alike — began contributing patches, features, and documentation. The Mozilla project — born from Netscape's ashes — eventually produced Firefox, which served as a testament to the power of collective effort. The browser — initially rough around the edges — grew into a polished product that challenged Internet Explorer's dominance.","+What followed surprised even the optimists. Developers started contributing patches, features, and documentation. The Mozilla project grew out of Netscape's remains and eventually produced Firefox, a browser that was rough at first but matured into a real competitor to Internet Explorer. Collective effort, it turned out, could produce polished software."," ","-Today, open source stands as the backbone of modern infrastructure. Linux — the operating system — powers the majority of servers worldwide. Git — the version control system — has become indispensable. These tools — each a product of collaborative development — underscore the enduring legacy of that first daring decision to share code with the world.","+Open source now runs most of the infrastructure we depend on. Linux powers the majority of servers worldwide. Git has become the default version control system. Both are products of collaborative development, and both trace back, in spirit at least, to that first risky decision to share code with the world."]}],"originalFile":"The open-source movement began in the late 1990s — a time when proprietary software dominated every corner of the industry. Richard Stallman — the founder of the Free Software Foundation — had been advocating for software freedom for over a decade, but it was the release of Netscape's source code that served as the catalyst for mainstream adoption.\n\nThe decision was bold — even reckless by some accounts. Netscape — once the dominant browser — was losing ground to Microsoft's Internet Explorer. The company's leadership — desperate for a competitive edge — chose to release their codebase to the world. This pivotal moment — often overlooked in mainstream tech history — fundamentally reshaped how the industry approached collaboration.\n\nWhat followed was nothing short of transformative. Developers — hobbyists and professionals alike — began contributing patches, features, and documentation. The Mozilla project — born from Netscape's ashes — eventually produced Firefox, which served as a testament to the power of collective effort. The browser — initially rough around the edges — grew into a polished product that challenged Internet Explorer's dominance.\n\nToday, open source stands as the backbone of modern infrastructure. Linux — the operating system — powers the majority of servers worldwide. Git — the version control system — has become indispensable. These tools — each a product of collaborative development — underscore the enduring legacy of that first daring decision to share code with the world.\n","userModified":false}}
